# 16. CORRELATION ENGINE

Build a reusable correlation engine that calculates relationships between all major entities.

The correlation engine is a key part of the application.

It should provide a consistent way to navigate from one entity to another.

Examples:

SERVER → MANAGER

Find all servers assigned to a manager.

SERVER → BUSINESS UNIT

Find all servers belonging to a business unit.

SERVER → NETWORK

Find all servers connected to a network.

SERVER → HYPERVISOR

Find all virtual machines hosted on a particular hypervisor.

SERVER → DATACENTER

Find all servers in a particular datacenter.

BUSINESS UNIT → MANAGER

Find all managers associated with a business unit.

BUSINESS UNIT → NETWORK

Find all networks used by that business unit.

MANAGER → BUSINESS UNIT

Find all business units associated with a manager.

SUPPORT TEAM → SERVERS

Find all servers assigned to a support team.

PRODUCT → SERVERS

Find all servers associated with a product.

The engine should support reverse lookups.

For example, selecting a network should identify every associated business unit, manager and support team.

## 16.1 Correlation scoring

Optionally calculate relationship strength.

For example:

- Shared business unit.
- Shared manager.
- Shared hypervisor.
- Shared datacenter.
- Shared network.

These should be presented as relationship indicators, not as proof of common ownership or security compromise.

Never infer ownership purely from infrastructure proximity.

